# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GimnasioLibreSV — TLS + reverse proxy with Caddy (automatic Let's Encrypt).
#
# Caddy is the only public entry point (ports 80/443). It reaches each gym's
# `web` container over the shared Docker network `gimnasiolibre-edge`, by the
# alias `<WEB_ALIAS>-web` (attached by docker-compose.prod.yml; default `gym-web`).
#
# Uncomment one block per gym and replace the domain. Then reload:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml restart caddy
#
# ── Single gym ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# gym1.example.com {
# 	reverse_proxy gym-web:80
# }
#
# ── Several gyms on one VPS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One stack per gym (own directory, own ./data, own .env with a unique
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME and WEB_ALIAS). Only ONE stack runs Caddy; the others
# start just their services. Example layout:
#
#   /srv/gimnasios/gym1/.env   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=gym1   WEB_ALIAS=gym1   ← runs Caddy
#   /srv/gimnasios/gym2/.env   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=gym2   WEB_ALIAS=gym2
#
#   gym1.example.com {
#   	reverse_proxy gym1-web:80
#   }
#   gym2.example.com {
#   	reverse_proxy gym2-web:80
#   }
#
# Caddy issues and renews each certificate on its own; the first request to a
# new domain can take a few seconds while it does. Certificates live in the
# `caddy_data` volume, so they survive restarts and upgrades.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Uncomment and edit — `gym1.example.com` is a placeholder, not a real site:
# gym1.example.com {
# 	reverse_proxy gym-web:80
# }
